{
  "id": "035-phantom-vibrations",
  "title": "Your phone did not buzz",
  "format": "short",
  "theme": "psychology",
  "voice": "af_heart",
  "speed": 1.0,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "You felt your phone buzz. You check. Nothing.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "phone"
        ],
        "label": "bzzz?"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "Nothing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "You're not alone. In one study, 89% of college students said it happened to them.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "number",
        "value": 89,
        "label": "of students felt phantom buzzes",
        "suffix": "%"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "89%"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Across studies, it's somewhere between about 30 and 89 percent of phone users.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "compare",
        "unit": "%",
        "left": {
          "label": "low estimate",
          "value": 30
        },
        "right": {
          "label": "high estimate",
          "value": 89
        }
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "30",
        "89"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It's called phantom vibration syndrome.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "phantom",
          "vibration"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "phantom"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "When you're expecting a message, your brain gets primed to detect one.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "brain"
        ],
        "label": "expecting a buzz"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "primed"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "So it mistakes other sensations, like a muscle twitch or your clothes moving, for a vibration.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "split",
        "left": "muscle twitch",
        "right": "phone buzz",
        "leftIcon": "person",
        "rightIcon": "phone"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "mistakes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "One researcher calls it a tactile hallucination.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "tactile",
          "hallucination"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "hallucination."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It usually starts within a month to a year of carrying a phone.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "timeline",
        "points": [
          {
            "year": "1 MONTH",
            "label": "it can start"
          },
          {
            "year": "1 YEAR",
            "label": "most have it"
          }
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "month"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "So next time you feel a buzz, ask yourself, was that the phone, or your brain?",
      "visual": {
        "type": "question",
        "label": "phone or brain?"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "brain?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "claim": "Drouin found 89% of undergraduates experienced phantom vibrations; studies range 29.6% to 89%; brain misreads sensations when anticipating a call; starts after one month to one year; Rothberg calls it a tactile hallucination",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phantom_vibration_syndrome"
    }
  ],
  "publish": {
    "title": "Your Phone Didn't Actually Buzz 📱",
    "description": "89% of students in one study felt phantom vibrations. Here's why your brain invents them.",
